anets-1.3734359" class="external text" id="mwDPs">Archived</a> from the original on 25 August 2016<span class="reference-accessdate" id="mwDPw">. Retrieved <span class="nowrap" id="mwDP0">5 June</span> 2017</span>.</cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Ajournal&amp;rft.genre=unknown&amp;rft.jtitle=Canadian+Broadcasting+Corporation&amp;rft.atitle=What+worlds+are+out+there%3F&amp;rft.date=2016-08-25&amp;rft_id=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.cbc.ca%2Fnews%2Ftechnology%2Fwhat-worlds-are-out-there-a-field-guide-to-exoplanets-1.3734359&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3APlanet" class="Z3988" about="#mwt159" id="mwDP4"></span></span></li>
<li about="#cite_note-42" id="cite_note-42" data-mw-footnote-number="42"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwDP8"><a href="#cite_ref-42" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwDQA"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwDQE">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-42" class="mw-reference-text reference-text"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt162" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwDQI" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Cite web ","href":"./Template:Cite_web"},"params":{"last":{"wt":"Chen"},"first":{"wt":"Rick"},"date":{"wt":"23 October 2018"},"title":{"wt":"Top Science Results from the Kepler Mission"},"url":{"wt":"http://www.nasa.gov/kepler/topscience"},"access-date":{"wt":"11 July 2022"},"website":{"wt":"NASA"},"quote":{"wt":"The most common size of planet Kepler found doesn&apos;t exist in our solar system—a world between the size of Earth and Neptune—and we have much to learn about these planets."},"archive-date":{"wt":"11 July 2022"},"archive-url":{"wt":"https://web.archive.org/web/20220711224819/https://www.nasa.gov/kepler/topscience/"}},"i":0}}]}'/><cite id="CITEREFChen2018" class="citation web cs1" about="#mwt162">Chen, Rick (23 October 2018). <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://web.archive.org/web/20220711224819/https://www.nasa.gov/kepler/topscience/" class="external text" id="mwDQM">"Top Science Results from the Kepler Mission"</a>. <i id="mwDQQ">NASA</i>. Archived from <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://www.nasa.gov/kepler/topscience" class="external text" data-mw-original-href="http://www.nasa.gov/kepler/topscience" id="mwDQU">the original</a> on 11 July 2022<span class="reference-accessdate" id="mwDQY">. Retrieved <span class="nowrap" id="mwDQc">11 July</span> 2022</span>. <q id="mwDQg">The most common size of planet Kepler found doesn't exist in our solar system—a world between the size of Earth and Neptune—and we have much to learn about these planets.</q></cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Ajournal&amp;rft.genre=unknown&amp;rft.jtitle=NASA&amp;rft.atitle=Top+Science+Results+from+the+Kepler+Mission&amp;rft.date=2018-10-23&amp;rft.aulast=Chen&amp;rft.aufirst=Rick&amp;rft_id=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.nasa.gov%2Fkepler%2Ftopscience&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3APlanet" class="Z3988" about="#mwt162" id="mwDQk"></span></span></li>
